    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks for that. Using \"unknown\" or \"anonymous\" makes sense for the high-level schema, but I'm also thinking about the actual descriptive side of things. Some of these maps have these very strange, specific markings or symbols in the margins that don't seem to correspond to any known cartographic icons of the period. They feel almost... intentional, but impossible to categorize. \n\nIf I can't find a standard controlled vocabulary term to describe these anomalous features or the mysterious origins of some of these plates, how much leeway do I have to include descriptive, qualitative notes in the metadata without compromising the \"standardized\" nature of the Dublin Core schema? I want to capture the nuance of these unidentified elements without making the data messy.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You have significant leeway to include qualitative observations for anomalous features by using standard Dublin Core fields designed for unstructured or descriptive data, such as `dc:notes`, `dc:experience`, or the `TopLevelConcept` vocabulary. To maintain standardization, you can still link these notes to a controlled vocabulary where possible, but applying general descriptors like \"anomalous\" or \"unidentified symbol\" is widely accepted practice for Archival Information Objects when specific ontologies are too rigid. This approach preserves the nuance of your findings while keeping the metadata machine-readable and compatible with future processors that value descriptive depth over strict controlled taxonomies.",
      "source": "target",
